# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework and Amazon ECS's best
practices, for this repository: one section per pillar, what the pillar asks
of a container service on Fargate behind a load balancer, what each tier
does about it, what `infra/operations.ts` changes, the CDK Nag rules
(`AwsSolutions` pack) and the Standard's checks that enforce it, and what
stays by hand, with the reason. Sources and the date they were read are in
`docs/style/SOURCES.md`. This file belongs to the Standard: a task never
changes it.

`make check` synthesizes every stack for both tiers, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and runs CDK Nag's
`AwsSolutions` checks on both (`infra/standard.ts`): a finding fails synth
unless it is acknowledged, on one construct, with a reason.
`infra/standard.test.ts` checks, for both tiers, what CDK Nag doesn't: tags,
wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies, and every
promise below. `cfn-lint --include-checks I` lints both, with I3011 (a
stateful resource without a deletion policy) and I3013 (content that expires
without a set retention).

## Tiers and the operations block

The tier is where the stacks run, and a deploy names it:
`cdk deploy -c tier=production`, or `development` when it names none. The
code never picks one, and nothing else is read at synth: a synth of a tier is
the same on every machine. A development stack's name ends in
`-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes a production
stack. `infra/standard.ts` (`TIER_RULES`) says what each tier means, and
the stack adds its NAT gateways:

| Every stack                                                          | `production`                        | `development`   |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------- |
| Data: the table, the backup vault and its key                        | kept when the stack goes (`RETAIN`) | deleted with it |
| Deletion protection on the table, and on the stack                   | on                                  | off             |
| Point-in-time recovery on the table                                  | on                                  | off             |
| AWS Backup                                                           | daily, for `retentionDays`, copied  | none            |
| X-Ray tracing, through a collector in each task                      | on                                  | off             |
| Log retention, and the access logs' expiry                           | one year                            | one month       |
| NAT gateways                                                         | one per zone, two                   | one             |
| Alarms, their topic, a dashboard, deployments that roll back on them | on                                  | on              |

What the Container promises in either tier is `infra/operations.ts`, which
the factory renders from its `spec.operations`; a change to the block is
written into the file, never the other way.

- `backups.retentionDays`: days AWS Backup keeps each daily backup in
  production; 0 keeps point-in-time recovery only.
- `backups.copyToRegion`: a second region every backup is copied to; empty
  for none.
- `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto`: the most data a restore may lose and
  the longest it may take, which `docs/runbook.md` is written to.
- `alerts.email`: the default of each stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which
  a deploy sets per environment; empty tells nobody.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stacks in `infra/` are the only way resources are
  made. No change by hand in the console.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` synthesizes and
  lints every change for both tiers; nothing here deploys.
- Observe (OPS08, REL06): structured JSON logs with the request id, a log
  group per container, the load balancer's access logs and the VPC's flow
  log, each kept for the tier's time, and Container Insights on the
  cluster. In production, OpenTelemetry traces of every request but the
  health checks, through the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry collector beside
  the app, in X-Ray.
  - `AwsSolutions-ECS7`: every container logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-ECS4`: the cluster has Container Insights.
  - `AwsSolutions-ELB2`: the load balancer has access logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC7`: the VPC has a flow log.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every log group keeps the tier's
    time, and that every task runs the collector where the tier traces and
    none elsewhere.
- Alarms are code (OPS08-BP04, REL06-BP03), the same in both tiers:
  `cdk-monitoring-constructs` builds them on the load balancer's own 5xx,
  the tasks' 5xx and p99 latency (over a second), the service's CPU and
  memory (over 90 %) and running tasks (under two), and the table's read
  and write throttles and system errors, each firing on one bad five-minute
  period; and on the share of 5xx, from the load balancer and from the
  tasks, over 5 % for three minutes in a row, and unhealthy tasks in two
  minutes of three, the ones a deployment rolls back on. Every alarm
  notifies the stack's one SNS
  topic, encrypted with its own key, and a dashboard per stack shows them
  (OPS08-BP05). `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every load balancer,
  target group, service and table has its alarms and that every alarm
  notifies the topic: a new resource gets its alarms from the stack's
  `Alarms`.
- Nobody is told without asking: the `AlertEmail` parameter subscribes one
  address to the topic, and an empty one none. The topic's ARN is the
  stack's `AlarmTopicArn` output and the load balancer's DNS name
  `EntryPoint`, so a deploy target can subscribe its own channel and point a
  domain at the service.
- Deploy safely: a deployment starts new tasks before stopping old ones
  (minimum healthy 100 %), and rolls itself back when the circuit breaker
  sees the new tasks fail to start or pass the health check, or when a
  deployment alarm fires (the 5xx-share and unhealthy-task ones,
  `ROLLBACK_ON_ALARM`; a stray 5xx only notifies).
- A runbook: `docs/runbook.md` says what each alarm means and what to do
  first, how to restore the data and how a deployment rolls back. A change
  to what the stacks keep updates it, as a Standard change.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested).
- By hand, and why: whether the alarms fire for the right reasons, and
  which are missing (REL06-BP06), only a deployed stack's history shows;
  review it after every incident.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Least privilege: the task role, which the code runs as, is granted
  through CDK grants, such as
  `table.grant(taskDefinition.taskRole, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`, only the
  calls the code makes, and in production X-Ray's two write actions; the
  execution role, which ECS uses to pull the images and write logs, only
  what CDK grants it.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role, but AWS
    Backup's, whose `AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForBackup` is acknowledged in
    `infra/backups.ts`.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why. X-Ray's write actions
    take no resource but `*`: `infra/standard.ts` acknowledges that, in
    production, on a policy where nothing else has it.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action anywhere, but a
    key's default policy, which hands the key's use to the account's IAM;
    no X-Ray writes where the tier doesn't trace.
- Protect the network: the tasks run in private subnets without public IPs
  and take traffic only from the load balancer's security group; only the
  load balancer faces the internet.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC23`: a security group open to `0.0.0.0/0`; the load
    balancer's is, as a public service's must be, and is acknowledged with
    that reason. No other group may be.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC3`: network ACLs are used sparingly; security groups
    do the work.
- Authenticate requests where the issue asks for it: the load balancer can
  authenticate users (an OIDC provider or Cognito) on its HTTPS listener, or
  the service checks a token itself. A route without authentication is a
  choice the issue states.
- Encrypt at rest and in transit.
  - Clients speak HTTPS to the load balancer, with TLS 1.3 and 1.2 only
    (`SslPolicy.RECOMMENDED_TLS`); port 80 only redirects. The load balancer
    drops invalid header fields.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`, `AwsSolutions-SQS4`, `AwsSolutions-SNS3`: requests
    must use TLS; the alarm topic refuses any other.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS2`, `AwsSolutions-EFS1`: encryption at rest; DynamoDB
    is always encrypted, and `infra/standard.test.ts` requires a KMS key
    (`SSESpecification`) on every table, encryption on every bucket, queue
    and topic.
  - Backups are encrypted with the vault's own key (REL09-BP02); the alarm
    topic with its own, which CloudWatch may use to publish.
  - `AwsSolutions-KMS5`: customer-managed keys rotate.
- Protect data from the public: `AwsSolutions-S2` (bucket public access
  blocked), `AwsSolutions-S5`, `AwsSolutions-EVB1` (no open event bus
  policy), `AwsSolutions-ECR1` (no open image repository).
  A WAF web ACL on the load balancer is for the issue that asks for it.
- Secrets live in Secrets Manager, rotated (`AwsSolutions-SMG4`), read at
  start-up or passed as ECS `secrets`; never in an environment variable, the template, a log or the
  repository.
  `AwsSolutions-ECS2` flags every plain environment variable: acknowledge
  it only when each one is plainly not a secret (a table's name, the
  collector's address), and say so.
- The containers run as unprivileged users on read-only root file systems;
  the app's image holds the bundle and Node, nothing else, and the
  collector's image is pinned by tag.
- The account's own guards (GuardDuty, Security Hub, CloudTrail, AWS Config)
  are its owner's, not a stack's: `docs/runbook.md` lists them.
- By hand: validate all input at the boundary; never log user data or
  tokens; never return internal errors to a client.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Multiple Availability Zones: the VPC spans two, the load balancer serves
  from both and the service keeps at least two tasks, which ECS spreads over
  them; DynamoDB spreads on its own. In production each zone has its own
  NAT gateway, so a lost zone leaves the other's tasks their way to ECR,
  CloudWatch and X-Ray; development's one NAT gateway is in one zone.
- Health checks: the load balancer checks `/health` and replaces a task that
  fails; the check reads nothing, so a DynamoDB outage doesn't stop every
  task.
- Scale with demand: the service scales on CPU between its bounds.
- Recovery objectives (REL13-BP01): `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto` say
  what a restore in production may lose and how long it may take;
  `docs/runbook.md` is written to them.
- Back up data (REL09), in production:
  - Point-in-time recovery on the table: any second of the last 35 days,
    up to about five minutes before now, restored to a new table. It meets
    an RPO of minutes.
  - An AWS Backup plan (REL09-BP03): a daily backup at 05:00 UTC of every
    resource carrying the Container's tag, so a new table is in it, kept
    `retentionDays` in a vault with its own key (REL09-BP02); with
    `copyToRegion`, each copied to a vault in that region (the
    `<name>-backup-copy` stack), so a lost region loses no more than a day
    (REL13-BP02, backup and restore).
  - No vault lock: in compliance mode it can never be undone, which is the
    account owner's decision.
  - The tasks hold no data, and the load balancer's access logs are logs,
    not data: they expire after the tier's log retention, their bucket is
    kept in either tier, as a bucket holding objects can't be deleted, and
    the backup plan leaves it out by its ARN, though it carries the tag.
  - Development backs up nothing: its data goes with its stack.
- Nothing of value is deleted by accident, in production: the table is
  `RETAIN` with deletion protection, and the stack has termination
  protection. A change that replaces the table (a new key) keeps the old one
  but leaves it outside the stack, empty of the new one's data: review
  every change to a table's key as a migration.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery; `infra/standard.ts`
    acknowledges it in development only.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: per tier, the removal policies, the
    protections, point-in-time recovery, the backup plan, its vault and its
    copy, and that no load balancer's log bucket is backed up.
  - cfn-lint I3011: every stateful resource has a deletion policy.
- Restore by a written procedure (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP05):
  `docs/runbook.md` restores the items to a point in time, from the vault,
  from the copy, and a deleted table, and says how a deployment rolls back.
- Handle failures in asynchronous work: a queue or event source has a
  dead-letter queue.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS3`: a queue has a DLQ or is one.
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice (whole-item puts,
  conditional writes, idempotency keys).
- Limits and shutdown: a body limit on every request; keep-alive longer than
  the load balancer's 60-second idle timeout; on `SIGTERM` the server stops
  taking requests and finishes those in flight, within the container's stop
  timeout, then sends the spans it holds; ECS sends it only after the load
  balancer has drained the task.
- By hand, and why: a restore drill (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP03), at least a
  quarter apart, and a game day once a year; only an account shows that a
  restore works and how long it takes, so the RTO is a promise until a drill
  measures it. `docs/runbook.md` says how.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest Node.js LTS in the image (`node:24-alpine`); a bump is a
  change to the `Dockerfile`.
- Size the task's CPU and memory to the work, and the service's scaling
  bounds to the load the issue states; measure before raising them. The
  app's share is the same in both tiers (0.25 vCPU, 512 MiB): in
  production the collector's own, 0.25 vCPU and at most 512 MiB, is added
  to the task, so the app is measured in development as it runs in
  production.
- Start-up: SDK clients created once, in `src/main.ts`; a small image
  (esbuild's bundle on Alpine, no `node_modules`), so a new task starts
  fast.
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a request path.
- Find where time goes with the production traces and the p99 latency
  alarm.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed service can show (start-up, scaling, latency in AWS).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- A service runs all the time, so it costs by the hour whether busy or not:
  two small tasks, the load balancer and the NAT gateways are the floor.
  Scale-out has a ceiling; DynamoDB is on demand.
- `arm64` (Graviton) tasks: cheaper per vCPU-hour than x86.
- The DynamoDB gateway endpoint is free and keeps table traffic off the NAT
  gateways, which charge per gigabyte.
- What production costs over development: a second NAT gateway, by the hour
  and by the gigabyte; tasks twice the size (0.5 vCPU and 1 GB instead of
  0.25 vCPU and 0.5 GB), half of it the collector's; X-Ray for every trace
  past the free tier (every request but the health checks); point-in-time
  recovery on the table (by its size, per month); AWS Backup's storage
  (each daily backup is a full copy of the table, kept `retentionDays`, so
  about that many times the table's size), and with `copyToRegion` the
  transfer of each copy and its storage there; twelve months of logs and
  access logs instead of one; and a KMS key per vault. The load balancer,
  the alarms (each per month) and the dashboard cost the same in both
  tiers. Lower `retentionDays` to pay less
  for backups; 0 keeps point-in-time recovery alone.
- Log retention limits what storage costs grow to (`infra/standard.test.ts`,
  cfn-lint I3013); the access logs expire from their bucket with it.
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container, once the
  account activates them as cost allocation tags.
- By hand, and why: right-size the task and the scaling bounds from
  measured load, which only a deployed service's metrics show; no interface
  endpoints or larger tasks unless the issue states the need.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- Fargate runs the tasks on shared infrastructure; scaling in to the
  minimum when idle keeps what runs to what the load needs.
- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention by tier, backups for
  `retentionDays`, development keeping no backups and tracing nothing, and
  no data stored that the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling, no work per request that can be done once at
  start-up, small images.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

## Acknowledging a finding

Fix the resource first. When the fix is wrong for this system, acknowledge
the one finding on the construct the finding's path names (or its nearest
ancestor you own): a finding on `Task/ExecutionRole/DefaultPolicy/Resource`
is acknowledged on the execution role, not on the task definition.

```ts
Validations.of(taskDefinition.obtainExecutionRole()).acknowledge({
  id: 'AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::*]',
  reason:
    'ecr:GetAuthorizationToken accepts only the resource "*"; every other action names its resource.',
});
```

The reason says why this resource needs it, true and specific; the reviewer
checks every one, and a vague reason ("not needed", "for now") is blocking.
