{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, for this repository: one
section per pillar, what the pillar asks of a service on EC2 instances in an
Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer, the CDK Nag rules (`AwsSolutions`
pack) that enforce it when `cdk synth` runs, and what you do by hand where no
rule does. Sources and the date they were read are in
`docs/style/SOURCES.md`. This file belongs to the Standard: a task never
changes it.

`make check` runs CDK Nag's `AwsSolutions` checks on every stack
(`infra/standard.ts`): a finding fails synth unless it is acknowledged, on
one construct, with a reason. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks what CDK Nag
doesn't: tags, wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stacks in `infra/` are the only way resources are
  made, the instances' setup included (`bootCommands`). No change by hand in
  the console, and no logging in to an instance to fix it: replace it.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` synthesizes and
  lints every change; nothing here deploys. A deployed change rolls through
  the Auto Scaling group one instance at a time.
- Observe: structured JSON logs with the request id, shipped by the
  CloudWatch agent to a log group; load balancer access logs in S3; rejected
  traffic in a VPC flow log; the group's metrics and the instances' detailed
  monitoring in CloudWatch.
  - `AwsSolutions-ELB2`: the load balancer writes access logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC7`: the VPC has a flow log.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC28`: detailed monitoring on the instances.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every log group has a retention.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested), and the Auto Scaling group passes them to
  its instances.
- By hand: alarms on 5xx responses, unhealthy hosts and latency, and a
  runbook, when the system's needs ask for them. Scaling notifications
  (`AwsSolutions-AS3`) are acknowledged until something subscribes to them.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Least privilege: the instances have their own role, granted through CDK
  grants (`table.grant(role, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`) only the calls the server
  makes, and `s3:GetObject` on its own code.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action that allows.
- Protect the network: isolated subnets with no route to the internet;
  AWS services through VPC endpoints; the load balancer internal and open
  only to the VPC; the instances open only to the load balancer; no SSH key
  and no inbound rule for administration.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC23`: no security group open to `0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0`.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC27`: every security group says what it is for.
- Protect the instances: IMDSv2 required (a request forgery can't read the
  role's credentials); the server runs as its own user under systemd with
  `NoNewPrivileges` and a read-only system; Amazon Linux 2023's newest image
  at every replacement.
- Encrypt at rest and in transit.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC26`: EBS volumes are encrypted.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`: the access log bucket refuses requests without TLS.
  - DynamoDB is always encrypted, and `infra/standard.test.ts` requires a
    KMS key (`SSESpecification`) on every table, encryption on every bucket,
    queue and topic.
  - Inside the VPC the load balancer speaks HTTP. Making it internet-facing
    is an issue's decision: it needs an HTTPS listener with a certificate,
    an authorizer in front of the routes, and an acknowledgement of
    `AwsSolutions-EC23` on its security group saying why the world may
    reach it.
- Protect data from the public: `AwsSolutions-S2` (bucket public access
  blocked).
- Secrets live in Secrets Manager, rotated (`AwsSolutions-SMG4`), read at
  start-up; never in the user data, the unit's environment, the template, a
  log or the repository.
- By hand: validate all input at the boundary; never log user data or
  tokens; never return internal errors to a client.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Two Availability Zones: at least two instances, one per zone, and the load
  balancer in both.
- Replace what fails: the Auto Scaling group uses the load balancer's health
  check, so an instance that stops answering `GET /health` is replaced;
  systemd restarts a server that exits.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS2`: the group uses ELB health checks.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS1`: the group has a cooldown.
- Scale with demand: a target-tracking policy on CPU between the minimum and
  the maximum the stack sets.
- Change safely: a rolling update replaces one instance at a time and keeps
  one in service; a stopping instance gets 30 seconds to finish its requests
  (deregistration delay, `SIGTERM`). The update sends no resource signals,
  so it can't stop a release whose instances fail their health check:
  signals need `cfn-signal` and a CloudFormation endpoint, an issue's
  decision when the system's needs ask for it.
- Back up data and recover it: point-in-time recovery on every table, and
  data that outlives its stack.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery.
  - `AwsSolutions-RDS10`: deletion protection on databases; set it on tables
    too (`deletionProtection: true`).
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: every stateful resource states its removal
    policy; data is `RETAIN`.
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice (whole-item puts,
  conditional writes, idempotency keys).
- Limits: a body size cap, keep-alive longer than the load balancer's idle
  timeout, and a request answered within it.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest Amazon Linux 2023 image and an LTS Node.js from its
  repositories.
- Size the instance to the work: measure before choosing a bigger type or a
  higher minimum.
- Start-up: SDK clients created once; the server bundled into one file by
  esbuild, so an instance starts without `npm install`.
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a request path.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed service can show (latency through the load balancer, scaling
  time).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- Instances, the load balancer and the Logs endpoint cost by the hour
  whether or not there is work: keep the minimum at two and the type small
  unless the issue states the load that needs more.
- No NAT gateway: gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB cost nothing, and
  one interface endpoint (Logs) costs less than a NAT gateway per zone.
- `arm64` (Graviton) instances: cheaper than x86 for the same work.
- On-demand DynamoDB, log retention, and access logs expired after 90 days
  limit what storage costs grow to.
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container.
- By hand: Savings Plans or Reserved Instances are the account owner's
  choice, not the stack's.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Scale in when demand falls: the target-tracking policy removes instances
  down to the minimum.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention, access logs expired, and no
  data stored that the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling, no work per request that can be done once at
  start-up.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

